# Chronicle stills

Every new cue still follows this. Do not re-ask. Do not dilute.

## Frame: horizontal 2:1 (default)

Every new still is **2:1 horizontal** unless the user names another shape (album covers stay 1:1; portraits/boards keep theirs).

- GenerateImage has no 2:1, so call it with `aspect_ratio: "16:9"` and compose for a 2:1 crop: keep heads, blades, hooves and the horizon inside the **central band** — the top and bottom ~6% are cut. Say so in the prompt ("composed for a 2:1 letterbox crop; nothing important at the top or bottom edge").
- The installers centre-crop to 2:1 (`scripts/crop-ratio.mjs`): `install-temp-art.mjs` (item `ratio` overrides; `"native"` keeps the generator frame) and `scripts/.cache/install-scene-stills.mjs <n> [ratio]`. Both accept the `.jpg` GenerateImage now writes — no png conversion.
- `add-image-slots.mjs` writes `ratio: 2` by default.

## Visual canon (source of truth — run it before every prompt)

Per-character and per-place visual facts live in **`src/lib/data/visual-canon.json`**: look, dress, demeanor, how they carry and fight, their horse by year, era changes, and a never-list. Plus armor kits, animals (`obj_*` boards) and place DNA. The builder **`scripts/visual-canon.mjs`** merges that with what already lives elsewhere — portrait-by-year, binyeo and object boards from `people.ts`, the character hex, sword tagline and pommel from `swords.ts`, place boards from `places.ts` — and prints refs plus a `CANON —` text block.

- `node scripts/visual-canon.mjs --year 660 --battle gyebek yushin place:hwangsan` → attach every printed ref, paste the text into the GenerateImage `description` (before the house suffix). `--battle` adds armor, sword, fighting and horse; `--sword` adds sword and fighting; `--mounted` adds the horse.
- `scripts/.cache/add-image-slots.mjs` does this automatically for manifest items with `people` (year from the entry; `canon: { battle, sword, mounted, with: ['gomanari', 'place:hwangsan'] }`, or `canon: false`).
- A recurring detail you learn (a grip, a scar, a horse, a crown) goes **into the canon**, not into one prompt. Never restate it by hand in a scene sentence that could drift.
- **Kingdom banners.** `--battle` (or `--flags`, or an explicit `flag:tang` id) attaches each side's `static/flag_*.png` (rasterized from `flag_*.svg` by `scripts/rasterize-flags.mjs`) and the banner text from `visual-canon.json` → `flags`. Battle stills show those banners on poles and pennants so each side's colour reads at a glance.
- **Horses are a centrepiece.** Any canon horse adds `horses.common`: full readable body, modelled muscle, edge light along the topline. Gesomun's horse is **Damul** (다물): red-bay, brown-red coat, black mane/tail/legs; battle stills attach the armoured `obj_damul_gaema.png` (Goguryeo 개마), otherwise `obj_damul.png`.
- **Tang palace.** `place:changan` attaches `pl_daming_night.png` (the night twin of `pl_daming.png`, painted from Daming Palace site photos and reconstruction models: Hanyuan Hall on red rammed-earth terraces, white balustrades, Dragon Tail ramps, que-towers on grey brick piers, grey tiles with owl-tail ridges). Lit and staged like a **Chinese historical drama** (*Legend of the Demon Cat*, *The Longest Day in Chang'an*): night or blue hour, hard backlight through doors and lattice, gold-yellow flame highlights against cool blue-green shadows, many practical flames in frame, red lantern spill, incense haze, reflective dark floors; human-scale drama blocking (officials in two inward-facing lines, banquet tables in two facing rows, emperor on a dais before a screen). Tang palette is vermilion + white with black-grey roofs trimmed in green glaze; yellow is the emperor's robe, not the roofs. Never the Forbidden City, never yellow glazed roofs, never the modern replica.
- **Costume charts and crowns by kingdom.** `visual-canon.json` → `costume` maps each kingdom to its hanbok chart: `ref_hanbok_silla.png`, `ref_hanbok_baekje_gaya.png` (Baekje and Gaya), `ref_hanbok_goguryeo.png` (also Buyeo and Jolbon), and `ref_hanbok_gojoseon.png`. The builder attaches the chart for every person of that kingdom and adds a `COSTUME CHARTS` line. Charts are style only: ignore their labels, poses and faces. For crowds with no named person, pass `dress:<kingdom>` (e.g. `canon: { with: ['dress:baekje'] }`). Anyone holding a King/Queen title that year, out of battle, also gets the kingdom crown: `ref_crown_silla.jpg` + `ref_muyeol.png` (the crown worn with the king's robe), `ref_crown_goguryeo.png`, `ref_crown_gaya.png`; Baekje is text-only (black silk cap with twin gold flame ornaments). Tang and Yamato have no chart.
- **Jougwan (조우관, feather cap).** Men of Silla, Goguryeo, Baekje and Gaya wear it by default: a small black lacquered cap on the crown with two long barred pheasant feathers, cords under the chin. The builder attaches `hat_jougwan_types.png` + `hat_jougwan_rider.png` (boards `hat_jougwan_*.png`; shape only, ignore their labels and museum props) and adds a `HEADWEAR` line. Hwarang keep the crescent-moon headband on the brow with the cap above it. It comes off in battle (helms), for anyone holding a King/Queen/Emperor title that year (crowns), for redressed eras, and for scenes passed `--no-hat` / `canon: { hat: false }` (bathing, sleeping, unclothed). A character `hat` field in `visual-canon.json` (`false`, `"jougwan"`, or a year-ranged list) overrides the default (Haemosu, Alji, Hogong are off). Dosuryu's portrait already wears one.
- **Era redress.** A canon era with `"redress": true` (Euija the prisoner from 660) makes the portrait face-only and drops the base look/dress, so the portrait's clothes and headband never leak back in.
- Signature fighting: **Gyebek** fights reverse-grip (역수) — ring above the thumb, straight blade back along the forearm, edge out. **Gesomun** carries five crow swords (four fanned on his back) and fights with **two at once**. **Yushin** orthodox forward grip, one sword. **Bidam** beads looped on the sword wrist. **Jumong** is an archer, not a duelist.

## Two layouts (pick one; never blend)

**Intimate / close** — personal, skin-forward, faces fill the frame. Sex and seduction stay here (not silhouettes). Painterly / anime-adjacent cinema. Not photoreal, not cartoon.

**Iconic / minimal epic** — zoomed out. Tiny figures *or* one isolated poster-scale body (lower third, silhouette behind a plane), hard color planes, haze, reflective floors, monumental emptiness. Monochrome plus **one** symbolic accent. Vast scale only in this mode. Reference vibe: red wall / black figures / white haze / one yellow shaft.

**Iconic means the layout**, not “delete the world.” One geometric device, few hues, emptiness, no clutter. The still must still look like a **movie frame of that actual scene** — a real Korean place a camera could stand in.

**Earth location stills** (Amnok, Ansi, Sabi, Seorabeol, Daeya, Pyongyang, battlefields, palaces, rivers, mountains, gates, granaries): show **recognizable period architecture and ground**. Stone block walls, *giwa* tiled munru pavilions, timber doors with iron bosses, wooden storehouses, packed earth, wet yards. Natural sky (clouds, moon, haze, weather). Simple, not busy.

**Same place, every still.** If the entry has a place avatar (`static/pl_*.png` — e.g. `pl_pyongyang_fortress.png`, `pl_daeya_fortress.png`, `pl_ansi.png`), **attach that file** as a location ref and keep the architecture: the same red two-tier munru, the same stone ridge, the same pond-and-pavilion, not a cousin fortress or an abstract wall. New scenes open with a **wide exposition** of that place image before closer beats. Do not invent a different capital.

Canon Earth-location DNA (prompt-style only — **never attach as a GenerateImage ref**): `fortress-gate-red-omen` / `fortress_gate_red_omen` — real Daeya *seongmun* (weathered stone, crenellations, curved *giwa* pavilion, heavy wooden doors), real snow/earth, two tiny spear-guards, **one** symbolic accent (the red seam). Iconic because of symmetry and the single accent — not because the building became a slab.

**Forbidden on Earth location beats** (these are the bad examples — never remake them this way):
- Abstract monoliths, brutalist stone slabs, color-plane “buildings” that are not buildings
- A granary as a burning rectangle / pillar with no roof, posts, or grain-doors
- A gate as a sci-fi block with no *hongye* arch, no *giwa* munru, no timber leaves
- Flat graphic void standing in for a fortress, palace, or yard

Flat void / hard color-plane is one tool for **otherworld** or a deliberately indoor/night-black beat — not the default for Korea. Sex/close stays intimate and skin-forward. No army catalogs, no palace furniture dumps.

Gods may be more realistic / numinous. Hyukgose and Alyoung stay photoreal as demigods. Otherworldly presence is skin, scale, and atmosphere — not a cheap colored rim-glow sticker around the silhouette.

## Mortal / divine render split (shared frames)

When a **mortal** and a **god/goddess** share one still, do not paint them in the same mode.

- **Mortals** (Kim men, officers, queens in their human register): slightly animated / painterly anime-adjacent — the house cinema look.
- **Divine** (steam-cavern goddesses Narim / Golhwa / Hyullé, and other gods): photoreal-leaning, numinous, skin that reads as real. The contrast inside one frame is the point: the mortal is drawn, the divine is present.

Sex/appetite stills stay intimate/close. Faces must *want*: open or bitten mouth, flush, blown pupils, wink, drool, out-of-breath, feral — not serene beauty.

## Cavern goddess heat (appetite stills)

**Cavern atmosphere over photoreal.** Numinous *environment*: spirit-steam, ember motes (Golhwa `#e86820`), floating leaf-light (Narim `#3d9e52`), water-luminescence in the pool (Hyullé `#2eb8c4`). Anime-painterly fantasy cinema — not a documentary wet-cave photo. Encode in every cavern-heat prompt: `magical cavern atmosphere, steam and motes, anime-painterly cavern spirit, not photoreal, no body halo`. “Magical aura” means the cave (steam, motes, wet-stone catch), **not** an outline glow around a person. **Do not** paint mortal Kim men with a hex glow. Oracle mortal/divine split still applies on pronouncement stills (glowing *eyes* only); appetite stills stay luminous-divine vs painterly-mortal.

**Men in the spring are unclothed.** Seohyeon, Yushin, Chunchu, Bupmin/Munmu — face ref `ch_*.png` is **face only**. Prompt must say: do not wear the portrait’s hanbok / court silk / armor / headband; wet loose hair; bare bathing body, wet muscular frame, nothing on but water. Goddesses keep house silk + binyeo.

**Signature postures (every heat still + narration).** Golhwa: legs spread wide apart — sitting, riding rock, lounging, facing men — default open invitation. On rock: chima hiked up around hips, thighs bare on wet stone, silk bunched. Sex/pleasure face, not a polite smile. Hyullé: legs tightly together, knees pressed, gaze down or aside — shy/wrecked, soaked, highest libido under silence. Contrast is the picture. Narim: elder-failing / breathless when a Kim looks at her kindly.

**Erotic manhwa face/panel grammar (appetite stills).** Borrow the grammar, not another IP. Bake into GenerateImage prompts: `manhwa panel, erotic comic framing, heavy blush, heart pupils, sweat drop, motion lines, climax expression`. Loud readable faces — ahegao-adjacent pleasure, not a cute smile. Panel/collage energy: multi-inset body crops around a center face. Motion in the steam (speed lines, fabric hitch, drip trails). Aftermath: spent, trembling, messy hair, hiked chima. Thought bubbles stay mosaic-censored and dirtier. Magical *cave* atmosphere (steam, motes), anime-painterly — not a photoreal porn photo, not a character outline glow.

## Oracle pronouncement (glowing eyes)

When the cavern goddesses **pronounce** (the hill speaking — not ordinary talk, not teasing), their **entire eyes** go solid glowing character color. The whole eye, not a highlight or a ring. Hexes from `CHARACTER_COLORS` in `src/lib/people.ts`:

- Narim `#3d9e52`
- Hyullé `#2eb8c4`
- Golhwa `#e86820`

Use this **only** on pronouncement stills. Ordinary appetite stills keep living eyes. If the eyes glow, the beat is the oracle. **Eyes only** — never a body aura, emissive skin, or bloom around the silhouette.

## Cavern shapeshift (First Kim + Bidam expulsion)

Before they settle, the steam-cavern goddesses may appear as foxes, crones, or mature maternal beauties. Those stills are **form-forward** (the animal or age is the picture). Keep each sister’s **element** readable at a glance: Golhwa fire `#e86820` (heat shimmer, ember light, steam off skin), Hyullé water `#2eb8c4` (drip, spray, soaked silk), Narim leaves/earth-wood `#3d9e52` (floating leaves, moss, vine-shadow). One accent each. Do not invent a new settled face: when they choose to be seen as young women, attach `ch_narim.png` / `ch_golhwa.png` / `ch_hyullé.png` and match those portraits. House silk after they settle: white jeogori + character-color chima. When hair/head is visible, also attach the matching `bn_*.png` (Narim `/bn_narim.png`, Golhwa `/bn_golhwa.png`, Hyullé `/bn_hyulle.png`).

**Bidam expulsion (not a Kim).** To drive Bidam out they shift into **large threatening elemental beasts**: Golhwa fire-tiger (ember `#e86820`), Hyullé water-serpent (cyan `#2eb8c4`), Narim moss-bear / earthen bulk (`#3d9e52`). Spectacle, not tame. If he copied the Kim wash he is unclothed in the spring — beads in hand, no headband; navy `#141C2E` as specular on beads/wet silk in the dark, not a navy glow aura.

## Color symbolism (real light, not a glow aura)

Read accents from `CHARACTER_COLORS` in `src/lib/people.ts`.

**The hex is the color of real light in a dark scene** — key bounce, specular on silk/armor/wet stone, a lamp, a shaft, a reflection on a floor. Crushed blacks + one hard key; that key / catchlight / bounce **reads the hex**. It is not a body halo, not magic outline, not neon-painting the figure, not floating color fog standing in for the person.

- **Allowed:** Bidam `#141C2E` as navy specular on beads and silk in tenebrism; Jumong `#e8563f` as a red lamp catching a cheek; Sosuno `#e8a04a` as a dusty-gold ground stamp or lamplight on timber.
- **Forbidden:** glow aura, emissive skin, bloom around the silhouette, “Bidam navy glow,” character-color rim-glow sticker, coloring the whole body in neon.
- **Exception (eyes only):** cavern goddess **oracle pronouncement** — entire eyes solid glowing character color. Not a body aura. Cavern **appetite** stills: numinous *steam/motes* in the cave; never hex-glow on mortal Kim men.

- Bidam `#141C2E` — navy void of the room; gold is sutra/lotus, not generic gilding.
- Yushin `#2A5FB8` — Confucian marshal blue in cloth peek and hard key; rites, rank, blank tablets — not beads.
- Chunchu `#D8258C` · Munmu `#C41E3A` · Hwanin `#F4F1E8`
- Jumong `#e8563f` · Sosuno `#e8a04a` · Pumsuk `#7EB8F0` · Maehwa `#8AAFA0`
- Heaven–Earth King `#C30000` + `#3E79E4` · Daebyeol `#3B6FBF` · Sobyeol `#C94040` · Hallakgungi `#8FBF8A`

**Garments stay portrait-true.** Jumong’s red silk already matches his portrait; Sosuno’s dusty-rose hanbok stays dusty-rose (`#e8a04a` is lamplight or a ground stamp, not gold robes). Geumwa’s court silk stays red-burgundy from `ch_geumwa.png` — do not gold-plate the Buyeo yard. Two people = two garment-true figures against crushed black. Never “paint the whole still that color.” Never dump the person as a luminous color blob.

**Earth / mortal / Korea locations** (Amnok, Ansi, Sabi, Seorabeol, battlefields, palaces, rivers, mountains): skies are **natural** — clouds, sun, moon, haze, weather as the scene needs. Not a flat graphic void unless the beat is deliberately indoor or night-black.

**Non-earth / otherworld only** — underworld, mythic flower fields, heaven, god-realms, Ibiga’s cloud-country: **no naturalistic sun/moon/cloud weather** as a kingdom-sky dump. Those places stay symbolic (flat color, numinous). Ibiga *is* the clouds; she is not an exception that bans weather on Earth.

Do not write “No clouds” on Earth prompts (including Haemosu–Yuhwa Amnok).

## Fashion

Face/body likeness: `static/ch_*.png` only. Do not invent faces. Do not attach prior stills as face refs.

**Clothing refs are the same files.** Attach `static/ch_*.png` for garments too — the portrait already encodes house dress (goddesses: white silky jeogori + character-color chima; Kim men: period court/military as shown). Prompt must say face *and* garments match the attached portrait. Do not invent coral wraps, fantasy robes, or generic silk sheets.

**Binyeo is required when hair/head is visible.** Attach the matching `static/bn_*.png` (binyeo-only boards — not clothing). Prompt must say the hair ornament matches the attached binyeo ref. Do not invent fantasy tiaras or wrong pins. Cavern triad: Narim `/bn_narim.png` (jade branch), Golhwa `/bn_golhwa.png` (coral-flame), Hyullé `/bn_hyulle.png` (teal wave).

## Kingdom battle armor (generals)

Battle stills: **FACE from `ch_*.png`**. Lamellar language from `static/ar_lamellar_steel.png` plus kingdom helm refs (`static/ar_*.jpg`) — not the portrait’s simplified scale shirt, not Japanese *kabuto*, not generic fantasy plate, not a gold-painted metal suit. Ignore diagram labels and watermarks on those refs.

**Plates are metallic gray steel.** Cloth peeks at collar, sleeves, skirt, sash, plume-cord — red, blue, or yellow by country, **most often the character hex** as the only color highlight (real fabric + specular, not glowing armor).

- **Silla** (Yushin, Alchun, Sadaham on campaign): steel lamellar; tall pointed *jonghyeongju* helm; cloth `#2A5FB8` (Yushin) or ice-blue `#6fa8ff` (Sadaham).
- **Baekje** (Gyebek): steel lamellar; cloth `#d9b13a` yellow peeking, red sash/skirt accent; not a yellow metal cuirass.
- **Gaya** (Muryuk): steel lamellar; tall conical helm (고깔); purple `#8B5CF6` cloth peek / rim only — not a purple cape dump.
- **Goguryeo** (Gesomun, Yang Manchun): steel lamellar; **red wing** shoulder-flaps `#C30000` / `#d0362f` as the cloth accent, not gold plate.

One poster-scale general or tiny figures. No army catalog. Vary dutch / worm’s-eye / aerial / OTS. Faces grimace, shout, snarl — not a standing armor catalog. High contrast, crushed black, one hard key. Not even daylight on a gold field.

## Kingdom 환두대도 (ring-pommel swords)

Every named Samhan blade is the **same sword** as `static/sw_bidam.png` except the **openwork inside the hollow ring**. Length, silhouette, and **handle:blade ratio** do not vary by owner.

Measured off `sw_bidam.png`: **handle : blade = 1 : 3** (ring + short grip + collars ≈ one-quarter of the whole; steel ≈ three-quarters). Lock that ratio in every still.

**Order along the axis (never reverse):**
small hollow RING (pommel — the *butt* of the handle, at the heel of the fist) → short gold band → short black diamond-wrapped GRIP → thin gold *collar* (a band, not a round guard) → long steel BLADE → point.

When the sword hangs down: RING is UP in the fist, BLADE is DOWN toward the ankle. The only circle on the whole sword is that small pommel ring. A second circle between grip and blade is a tsuba — forbidden. Grip is one closed fist, not a two-handed wrap.

- **Scale.** One-handed hip sword. Hilt at the hip → tip near the ankle. Never taller than the person. Never a two-handed greatsword or polearm.
- **Blade body — the one reference.** `static/sw_bidam.png` is the master board for every sword in the chronicle (ring pommel at the butt, 1:3 handle:blade). Attach it on **any still where a sword could be in frame** — a duel, a general, armored cavalry, a scabbard at the hip, a sword in the background, a sword one inch tall. No exceptions for "small" or "silhouette" or "not the subject"; a blade drawn without it drifts (katana, tsuba, jian, ring in the wrong place). Paste `SWORD_STILL_ANATOMY` from `src/lib/swords.ts` into the prompt with it. `visual-canon.mjs --battle/--sword` does both; `add-image-slots.mjs` adds both automatically when the scene text mentions a sword/blade/hilt/scabbard. Hand-written GenerateImage calls must do it by hand. Ignore swords drawn on `ch_*.png` (Yushin’s portrait katana; Kangrim’s smear-blade).
- **When the blade is the subject** (close duels, sword-on-knees, held-up offerings), a portrait with a drawn katana (`ch_kim_yushin.png`, `ch_gyebek.png`, `ch_echi.png`) still leaks a curve and a tsuba even when told to ignore it. Drop that portrait, describe the face from `visual-canon.json` in words, and call the weapon “the ancient straight Korean ring-pommel dao of the attached sw_bidam board: plain small iron ring pommel, short grip, thin metal band, NO hand guard of any kind, perfectly STRAIGHT narrow blade like a ruler.” That phrasing holds where “tsuba forbidden” does not.
- **`sword_*.png` is a pommel close-up, not a second sword.** Attach it only when the ring itself is the shot (ECU). On full-blade stills, **do not attach it** — the model reads that giant circle as a *tsuba* in the middle of the blade (handle/blade in the wrong order). Name the motif in the prompt instead (fish, horse, crow, lotus, dragon, chrysanthemum, fox) and keep the ring as small as the one already on `sw_bidam.png`.
- **Forbidden:** ring at the blade tip; a large disc between grip and blade; katana / *tsuba*; short jian; ball pommel; a new sabre length; oversized steel filling the frame.
- **Exceptions (not this type):** Xue Rengui’s *fangtian ji*. Clan-tourney *mokgeom* (wood). The Seven-Branched Sword as a unique relic. Gyebek’s phoenix lives **in the ring**, not as a different curve; his reverse grip is a hold, not a different sword.

**Cavern elemental triad** (composition, not a lecture): Golhwa = fire (heat shimmer, ember `#e86820`, steam off skin). Hyullé = water (drip, spray, soaked `#2eb8c4`). Narim = leaves / earth-wood (moss, floating leaves, vine-shadow `#3d9e52`). One accent each; monumental emptiness still.

Garments: striking hanbok (or period court/military dress in that language). Cloth reads **silky and premium** — sheen, weight, clean drape — not matte rags, not photoreal costume dumps. Few hues, high contrast, **one** accent. Fashion is symbolism, same as the color planes.

Not busy embroidery catalogs. Not generic fantasy robes.

- **Intimate / close** — hanbok/period still; skin-forward; silk, not cluttered extras.
- **Iconic / minimal epic** — even tiny figures read as hanbok silhouettes (wide sleeves, jeogori/chima or court rank robes), one color + void, silk catching the one accent light. Poster-scale bodies wear the same silky, minimal dress.

## Prompt style (canon batch)

House voice for every GenerateImage `description` and every new `prompt` field. Distilled from the iconic-poster batch (GenerateImage calls that produced `wuzetian_screen_power`, `yushin_sword_vertical`, `chunchu_strategist_shadow` — plus siblings Gyebek yellow-plane, Gesomun low-angle, Euija light-seam). Those files are **prompt-style only — never attach them as GenerateImage refs**.

Winning formula (paraphrase into each call; do not paste old filenames as refs). Poster batch plus the **Jumong remake batch** that landed (high contrast, named device, dramatic pose, hex as real-light accent):

`Minimal iconic [ratio] still. [CINEMATOGRAPHY — name the shot: dutch angle / worm’s-eye / bird’s-eye / over-shoulder / ECU / two-shot]. [LENS: shallow DOF, bokeh, rack-focus, long-shadow key]. Mise-en-scène: [one locked Korean place, few props]. ONE device: [named geometry that divides the frame]. [Name], [verb pose — mid-stride, kneel, full-draw, weigh, tumble], not a standing portrait clone. FACE ONLY from the attached portrait. [Character hex] as the hard key / bounce / specular in a dark scene — lamp, shaft, silk catch, floor reflection — NOT a glow aura, NOT emissive skin, NOT a halo. Striking silky [hanbok / court-military], few hues, graphic color-blocking. Earth: REAL Korean [gate / granary / river / hall] — stone, timber, giwa, packed earth — crushed blacks, one hard key, long shadows, chiaroscuro. Otherworld: symbolic flat color. No army. No palace clutter. No even daylight postcard. No text. No watermark. Painterly anime-adjacent, monumental.`

Reusable DNA:

- Open with **Minimal iconic** `{ratio}` poster/still (`2:1` unless told otherwise). NOT photoreal. NOT busy.
- **Name the cinematography.** Every prompt picks a shot (dutch, worm’s-eye, bird’s-eye, OTS, ECU, two-shot) and a lens/light term (bokeh, shallow DOF, rack focus, chiaroscuro, contre-jour, catchlight). Mise-en-scène is the locked place — not a lecture, one set. “Rim” means a hard key grazing silk — not a colored glow outline.
- **Foreground / midground / bokeh.** Sharp foreground (a shoulder, bucket, rope, sleeve), the beat in the midground, background *giwa* / timber melting into creamy bokeh. Rack-focus. This is the Jumong–Sosuno well-yard language — keep it.
- **One of each named person.** Never clone Sosuno, Jumong, or anyone twice in one still.
- **Wells are architecture.** Bodies stand or kneel on packed earth at the **rim**. Nobody stands inside the shaft.
- **Jumong’s face is fun.** Laid-back, grins, laughs, winks, easy eyes when Sosuno is mean. Not a grim founder statue.
- **Pre-king Jumong is clean-shaven.** No mustache, no goatee, no chin hair until he is king (`ch_dongmyung`). The exile portrait `ch_jumong.png` may show a small mustache — **ignore it**. Every pre-crown still: clean-shaven, no facial hair.
- **One graphic device is the whole composition.** From that batch (paraphrase, do not copy as image refs): vertical divider (blade as a hard black line top-to-bottom; figure in the lower third); film-noir half-face shadow, contemplative power; a translucent screen/plane with a single gold light through it (silhouette, influence without the throne). Also: hard light-seam, low-angle monument, tiny body in a vast empty hall.
- Face matches the **attached** `ch_*.png` portrait (or a face-suggestion / silhouette of that portrait).

## Heewon style (the house look — name it in every prompt)

**Webtoon figures × watercolor-and-oil world × simple minimal iconic layout × movie tenebrism.** The text lives in `HEEWON_STYLE` (`src/lib/promptHouse.ts`) and leads the house suffix; open every GenerateImage `description` with “HEEWON STYLE”.

- **Lead benchmark: `hadong-empty-saddle`** (prompt-style only, never a ref) — Chunchu, arm bound, walks down a storm-lit grass ridge toward his red bay tied to a stake with an empty saddle. One ridge diagonal, two subjects on the thirds, half the frame granulated storm sky, one cloud-break shaft, a tiny line of banners dissolving in haze, magenta blooming into the wash at the lower-left edge. That balance — painterly world, drawn webtoon figures, almost nothing else in frame — is the target.
- **Figures = Korean webtoon / anime.** Clean digital-ink linework, simplified expressive webtoon faces, shaded in soft watercolor washes and translucent oil glazes (not flat cel fill). No pores, no photographic detail.
- **World = watercolor first, oil second.** Granulated wet-in-wet washes and short Turner broken-colour dabs for sky and ground, dry-brush grass/dust/stone, soft bleeding edges, paper grain; the scene's accent colour blooms into the wash near one frame edge and the edges dissolve to bare paper. Oil only deepens the darks (umber-indigo glazes) and adds a touch of impasto on the brightest highlight. Heavy Baroque oil as the whole render is the drift to avoid **outdoors** (interiors and night scenes are the exception below).
- **Interiors and night = old-master canvas.** Benchmarks: `six-steeds-saluzi`, `six-steeds-qingzhui` (prompt-style only). The webtoon figure is painted into a European old-master canvas. Use Renaissance/Baroque tenebrist oil (broken-colour brushwork, glazed darks, sfumato, impasto on the brightest highlight) over faint paper grain. That texture covers **everything, the robes included**: mottled plaster, gritty stone, and silk brushed in loaded strokes with dry-brush drag along each fold, shaded in 3–4 painted value planes with soft broken edges. Only faces and hands keep clean ink line. The words that kill it: anything from 3D rendering ("specular sheen", "core shadow", "terminator", "bounce", "sculpted volume", "3D") and "soft watercolor washes" on the figure. The model answers those with smooth digital shading (`six-steeds-empty`, second aura take).
- **Interiors = tenebrist room in the same wash-and-glaze** (Caravaggio, La Tour, Rembrandt): umber-to-black ground; walls, timber, tables pulled out of the dark by one lamp / window / shaft. The webtoon figures sit *inside* that painted space under the same key — never pasted on a flat backdrop.
- **Minimal iconic layout.** One device (ridge diagonal, road, waterline, hall axis, table ring, gate), one or two subjects on the thirds, at least half the frame quiet negative space, distant detail tiny and dissolving. No crowds, no clutter.
- **Also good:** `duel-last-line-strip` (Gyebek under a Turner storm of broken-colour dabs, one golden shaft).
- “Renaissance oil” / “oil sketch” as the *whole render* drags faces into photoreal — the failure mode on the first Hwangsan oils. The paint belongs to the world and the shading; the faces stay drawn.
- **Layout = Romanticism** (Delacroix, Géricault, Friedrich, Goya). Heroic diagonal or pyramid; small figures against a sublime storm sky or vast field; extreme foreshortening — worm’s-eye from the dirt, or straight down from above. A level camera on a posed group reads as a costume photo.
- **Light = film tenebrism.** Big dark masses (storm cloud, shadowed slope, umber room, black floor) against one bright break that lands on the subject; inky darks beside small near-white highlights, few middle greys on the subject. One hard directional key — lamp, single cloud-break, low sun shaft, firelight — carves the subject out and *models* it: light wraps cheekbones, plates, horse muscle, silk folds, then drops off at a hard terminator. A thin edge of the same real light grazes the far side of the silhouette to separate it from the background (never a glow outline). Long raking shadows.
- **Depth = three planes, always.** Dark out-of-focus foreground shape near the lens (shoulder, rein, post, grass, smoke); subject razor-sharp in the midground; background dissolving into wash and atmospheric haze, with round painted **bokeh** discs from small real lights behind the subject (lamps, torches, embers, sparks, rain, wet highlights). Shallow DOF, overlapping shapes, receding haze. Never flat, never every plane in equal focus. A storm sky with one pale break, or one shaft, is the key — a real cloud opening, never a halo behind a head. Default hour is dusk, night, storm or pre-dawn; flat overcast, even daylight and a bright open sky filling the frame are failures.
- **Form = 2.5D (the premium look).** Lead benchmark: **`six-steeds-saluzi`** (prompt-style only, never a ref) — Taizong standing at the Saluzi relief, worm's-eye from the floor beside an oil lamp set down on the stone, palm on the carved neck, one held-back tear, his shadow climbing the wall. Drawn webtoon line outside, painted volume inside: every yellow-silk fold protrudes, with the lit side in one opaque loaded stroke, a crisp painted turn and the shadow in a thin umber glaze. Two things make it, and both are required: the **old-master canvas** texture (above) and the **scene staging** below. Describe folds in painter's words, never in 3D-render words. Write the staging into every scene sentence:
  1. **A practical light in or just outside the frame, close to the subject** (a lamp on the floor, a brazier, a candle in the hand), named with its position.
  2. **The light rakes across the surfaces the camera sees**, from below or the side. Never from behind the subject.
  3. **The subject large in frame** (medium or closer, worm's-eye or low three-quarter) so folds, beard and knuckles resolve.
  4. **Walls and floors at an angle to the lens**, so the cast shadow climbs a receding surface.
  5. **A sharp practical light in the foreground plus bokeh**, giving three planes.
  6. **Darkness as falloff across the body.** Never ask for a "near-black silhouette" or say "only the edge catches light" on a hero: the model answers with a flat cel-filled shape.
  - **Counterexample: `six-steeds-empty` (second aura take).** The staging was right and the folds had volume, but the prompt said "watercolor-and-oil world" and used 3D-render fold words. It came out clean and digital, with no brush texture.
  - **Counterexample: `six-steeds-empty` (first aura take).** Shot from directly behind, with the lamp in front of him and a wall flat to the lens. His robe came out as one flat mustard shape, with no protrusions.
- **Tenebrism benchmarks — the animal-scene batch** (prompt-style only, never refs): `bisamun-beads-bridle` (one lamp, black void, specular on beads), `nangbi-naming` (single camp lamp on a white coat), `bisamun-north` (navy dusk, one silver break). Flat-light counterexamples from the same batch were retaken.
- **Benchmarks — the watercolor Hwangsan batch** (prompt-style only, never refs): `duel-oil-pumil-pieta` (painted storm, granulated dust, drawn faces), `duel-report-ots` (Rückenfigur, wash-dissolved frame edges), `duel-horse-eye` (oil-glazed darks), `duel-oil-gwanchang-helm` (worm’s-eye watercolor sky), `duel-oil-duty` (top-down pool of light on painted ground), `duel-last-line-strip` (Turner storm). Layout-only: `duel-oil-kangrim-above` (*di sotto in sù* angle — not its oil render).
- This is the default for every new still unless the beat is an ink-painting hybrid or an explicit parody.

## Face refs are faces — not poses (do not dilute)

`ch_*.png` is **face + garment language only**. Every still invents a **new body and camera**. Forbidden: 3/4 standing fashion plate, clasped-hands clone, tiger-pelt portrait stance pasted onto a porch, two people side-by-side in the yard like a still from the character sheet.

Dynamic bodies only: mid-stride, kneel, full-draw, turn, tumble, lean, count, worm’s-eye, over-shoulder, dutch. **Name the pose as a verb** (arriving, weighing, splitting, diving). Wides are still **iconic** — one named device (hall as a dark wedge, pine as a vertical, arch as a leaf-light stamp, tiny figure in lower third) — not a tourist postcard of buildings with even daylight and a copied stance.

**High contrast is mandatory** on Earth wides too: crushed shadow masses, one hard key, long shadows, tenebrism. Not an even wash. Not a flat game-map establishing shot.

**Color symbolism is layout + real light.** Few hues. The character hex tints the **one hard key** (bounce, specular, lamp, shaft) — not a body aura. Two people = two garment-true figures against crushed black; each person’s hex may live in their catchlight, not as twin neon outlines. No muddy same-hue wash.
- **One** character-color accent with hex (from `CHARACTER_COLORS`) as real light in the dark. Earth: that light lives in a simple real place (lamp, wet stone, silk sheen). Otherworld: the rest may be a flat charcoal/black void — still not a person-halo.
- Monumental emptiness. No army, no palace clutter, no hall furniture. Earth skies are natural weather; otherworld skies stay symbolic.
- **Earth locations stay buildings.** Paraphrase the red-omen gate (do not attach it): real *seongmun*, real yard, one accent. Never a brutalist slab, never a burning monolith labeled “granary.”
- Graphic color-blocking, anime-painterly, not photoreal, not cartoon.
- Close with: No text. No watermark.

Do **not** copy those three devices (gold translucent screen / vertical sword that bisects / film-noir half-face). Invent a new one (ribbon, wedge, broken arc, edge-gaze, low strip, stamp, shaft).

Intimate stills use the same voice (one accent, silk, void, no clutter) but faces fill the frame.

## Movie sequence (Jumong / Pumsuk / Wu halls)

Grounded film: **same place**, clear chronology, variety of angles, stills locked to dialogue/beats. Open with a wide of that actual Korean place, then cut closer. Every frame still obeys the Jumong remake grammar: high contrast, one named device, dramatic pose, hex as real-light accent (not a glow). Do not flatten a sequence into even-daylight establishing postcards.

## Action frames (manga)

Fight, charge, chase and cut beats use **manga action-frame grammar** on top of the Heewon look; wides and quiet beats stay painterly. The text lives in `MANGA_ACTION` (`scripts/.cache/manga-action.txt`). Paste it into the prompt.

- **Angles.** Extreme foreshortening, with a blade, hoof or fist coming at the lens. Hard dutch tilt. Worm's-eye from the dirt under the hooves, or straight down from above. Eye-level coverage is a failure on an action beat.
- **Lines.** Bold dry-brush sumi-ink speed lines along the motion, radial focus lines converging on the face or the blade, and motion smears on limbs, mane and cloth. Add flung ink-splatter dirt.
- **Impact frame.** At the moment of contact, use a near-monochrome inverted flash: white figures cut out of black, one gold accent.
- **Never.** Panel borders, speech bubbles, onomatopoeia or sound-effect lettering, or any text. Faces stay webtoon ink line.

## Doctrine in the picture (do not lecture in captions)

- **Bidam is Buddhist** — meditates, recites, 108 beads, lotus, incense through the Radiance siege. Nativist *and* a man of this soil’s sutras.
- **Yushin is Confucian** — duty, yard, queen’s arithmetic. He stands; he does not sit in lotus.

## Unique layouts only

Splits, discs, ribbons, worm’s-eye, corner crops, low strips. Never reuse court-kowtow, egg/horse circle, or crescent-halo hwarang. Do **not** attach those photos, the Xue fortress-escape still, or `wuzetian_screen_power`, `yushin_sword_vertical`, `chunchu_strategist_shadow` (or their temp png/jpg / kebab-case copies) as GenerateImage refs. Face refs: `static/ch_*.png` only.

No readable text, no watermarks, no Latin captions on the image.

## Pipeline

1. Slot must already exist on the entry in `src/lib/data/story.json` (`add-image-slots.mjs` writes it with the canon block + house suffix).
2. Cursor `GenerateImage` (`aspect_ratio: "16:9"`, composed for 2:1) → `{slot-id}.jpg` in the conversation assets folder, attaching every canon ref — and `sw_bidam.png` whenever a sword can appear.
3. `node scripts/install-temp-art.mjs <manifest.json>` → centre-cropped 2:1 `static/temp/{id}.jpg` + `tempImage` + `ratio`. The script **deletes** the source file.
4. `scripts/sync-temp-art-inventory.mjs` if you skip the installer.

Manifest item: `{ "id", "alt", "prompt", "ratio"? }` (`ratio` defaults to 2).
